 "annotations": [
  {
   "id": "note-row1",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -190,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "INITIAL WIZARD\nTwo steps, then out of the way: a fact about the hardware, then what you came to do.\nThe gates share one modal shell - requirements as rows, the status mark only appears once met."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row2",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 990,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "OVERVIEW\nMost installed to least. The header's two slots carry it: stock firmware (bank 1), then Retro-Go (bank 2); a missing side dims rather than disappearing.\nEvery empty bank offers the step that fits THAT state, never a generic install."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row3",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 2170,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "GUIDED SETUP\nDone / active / pending read at a glance, and the header above tells the same story."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row4",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 3350,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "FIRMWARE SETUP - ADVANCED\nRail order, left to right. Firmware management, then flash management - each item one route, one file.\nCross-model runs one scale: gold informs, oxblood blocks. In flash management the bars are a shortcut that writes into the range fields; the fields are the truth."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row5",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 4530,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "REPOSITORIES\nOne row per project, grouped by kind, curated tagged. An emulator states what it PROVIDES - that is what lets it activate itself when matching ROMs appear."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row6",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 5790,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "ROMS\nCoverflow keeps the room. Budget and the one action live in the shared 72px footer."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-busy",
   "x": 4680,
   "y": -190,
   "w": 700,
   "text": "Unplugging mid-write corrupts the install, so the warning lives in the chrome and cannot be dismissed.\nIt clears on a safe-to-disconnect state, not when the progress modal closes - which is why the third state exists."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row7",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 9370,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "STOPPING, AND FAILING\nCancel is a text link, never disabled: the writer works in 256 KB blocks and only the erase inside one is unsafe, so it takes effect at the next boundary. A control that blinked on and off every half second could not be trusted.\nFailure is the end of the retry-twice rule. The busy chrome drops the moment the write stops - a page still saying do not disconnect over a dead flash would be lying. Blocks are named by what they carried first, address second."
  },
  {
   "id": "note-row8",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 10810,
   "w": 760,
   "text": "SPECIMEN\nNot a screen. Every --fs-* and every grey in tokens.css, rendered at the size and on the ground it is actually used on.\nThe near-identical values are drawn adjacently on purpose: if two tokens cannot be told apart here, they cannot be told apart in the app either."
  }
 ],
 "launch": {
  "view": "canvas"
 }
}